{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass, when you grow restless (תָּרִיד), that you shall break his yoke.</b> The word תָּרִיד is a foreign word in Scripture and does not sit well with Rashi's commentary, for the verse does not explain what the distress is. And there are those who explain it as an expression of dominion and rule, and they explain in it what they explain. And I say that this is its meaning: for Isaac knew through the holy spirit that was upon him that the blessing of 'and the elder shall serve the younger' (Genesis 25:23) would not obtain except at a time when Israel merit — and perhaps he understood this from what was said to him: 'from your womb they shall be separated' (Genesis 25:23) — this one to his wickedness and this one to his wholesomeness, and then 'the elder shall serve the younger,' for this righteous one rules over the wicked one through fear of HaShem. However, if he does not merit, why should he rule over him? And your mind should conclude that at a time when Israel do not merit, not only will the elder not serve the younger, but even the opposite — the elder will rule over the younger. Therefore he said that the matter is not so; rather, at a time when the younger merits and turns to his wholesomeness, you shall be a servant to him, as it is written: 'and your brother you shall serve' (Genesis 27:40). 'And it shall come to pass, when you grow restless (תָּרִיד)' — that is to say, there will come a time when you will be the ruler and dominate the entire world, and this is at a time when Israel do not merit, for when this one falls that one rises — then it is enough for you that you break his yoke from off your neck; but in any case, even you will not be able to dominate him. Therefore it is said in Obadiah (Obadiah 1:10): 'for the violence against your brother Jacob, shame shall cover you,' and it is written (Obadiah 1:21): 'and saviors shall go up on Mount Zion to judge Mount Esau.' From all this it is implied that Esau is destined to give account for having dominated Israel, for he was not given permission to dominate him, but only to break his yoke from off his neck."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:44.743791218Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}